PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2} 
Colleagues 5 and 7 correctly identify B2 as the common liberty that simultaneously puts C1-C2 into atari while threatening the B3-B4 chain. Following 5's line, after W B2 the only reply is B B1 (else immediate capture at B1); then W A1 is a genuine double capture removing both the C1-B1-C2 stones and the A2 stone in one move, leaving the B3-B4 group with only A4. This sequence is forcing regardless of Black's later replies on the right side. 

By contrast, the B1 supporters (1,8,9) overlook the point raised by 3 and 6 that B1 itself leaves A1 as a legal reply for Black in some lines, allowing Black to connect or capture the new white stone before the net closes. B2 avoids that ambiguity and directly initiates the shortage-of-liberties sequence. Other candidates such as A1 or A3 remain immediate atari and are refuted by the same liberty-counting logic. B2 therefore remains the unique first move that guarantees the kill.

